00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.160
So, authentication
versus authorization.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.980
Those of you who came
in here on an airplane

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:34.900
when you got to an
airport somewhere,

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.300
you had to produce a
passport, and that passport

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:38.560
is identification.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.225
And it is used, essentially,
to authenticate you.

00:02:42.225 --> 00:02:44.260
An authentication
token is a token

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:48.340
that is generated by
someone that we trust,

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:52.090
an authority that we trust,
and positively identifies

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:54.227
an individual or an API call.

00:02:54.227 --> 00:02:55.810
And it's different
than authorization.

00:02:55.810 --> 00:02:57.531
An authentication
just says this is me.

00:02:57.531 --> 00:02:58.780
It's like your identification.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.000
This is me and this is who I am.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.770
It alone doesn't enable
you to do things,

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:05.020
but then it can be
used to authorize you.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:07.374
So for example, if you've
got that in a visa,

00:03:07.374 --> 00:03:09.040
you might be allowed
to enter a country.

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:11.620
If you've got a driver's
license, that identifies you.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:14.050
It's authentication,
but it also authorizes

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:19.030
you to operate a motor vehicle
in the state of California.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:21.880
So what we're talking today
is about authentication,

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.120
and the key thing to note
there is that you cannot do

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:28.154
authorization if you
haven't done authentication.

00:03:28.154 --> 00:03:30.820
I can't decide if you're allowed
to walk through that door right

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:34.660
now if you don't have a badge
on that tells me who you are.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.770
So today we're going to be
talking about authentication.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:38.270
Now.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:39.780
When might you use this?

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:42.300
Well, how many people have
developed mobile APIs?

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:43.760
How many have mobile apps?

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.630
Those mobile apps make calls
back to an API frequently.

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:49.550
That's one of the
primary use cases

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:52.680
that we've seen for endpoints
over the last couple of years.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:55.430
But what we've learned in
talking to our customers is

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:58.250
that they've got a mobile
API, and that's-- we'll talk

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:00.020
a little bit about
user auth later.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:03.090
But behind that, almost
always, more frequently

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:04.840
than we thought, there
are other services.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.940
That mobile API that received
that call from the mobile app

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:11.450
is then making calls to
other backend systems.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.216
So there are ways to
authenticate users

00:04:13.216 --> 00:04:15.090
and we'll talk a little
bit about them later.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:17.149
But you've got something
in your mobile app that's

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:18.732
going to generate a
credential and you

00:04:18.732 --> 00:04:20.709
want to send that
over to your backend,

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:24.820
and have that thing
validate that auth token,

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:27.370
and validate that you
know who the user is

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:29.000
that made that call.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.670
But the issue is, that token
that was generated to make that

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:34.760
call, most frequently,
you don't want

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:36.830
that to be the kind
of authentication

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:39.130
that you use on those other--

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:40.910
that your mobile API is calling.

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:43.664
You actually would like those
services to reject that token.

00:04:43.664 --> 00:04:45.080
And the reason you
want to do that

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:50.250
is that you don't want that
guy, who has the ability

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:52.050
to get a hold of
one of those tokens,

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:53.870
to be able to call
your backend systems.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:56.550
Generally, they're backend
systems for a reason,

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.422
and you don't intend for them
to be exposed externally.

00:04:59.422 --> 00:05:00.880
You don't want
people calling them.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:02.796
So you need actually to
have different on auth

00:05:02.796 --> 00:05:04.720
than you do on
that front end API.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.560
And if you're using App
Engine, or App Engine Flexible

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.890
Environments, all of
those IP addresses

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:12.480
are visible from the internet.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:14.850
And so you need something
to be able to protect it

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:16.920
and to take a different
auth token then

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:20.220
you're sending from
your mobile app.

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:22.446
Anyone has ever heard
the word microservices?

00:05:22.446 --> 00:05:23.820
Yes, you've been
in this industry

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:24.690
for the last two years.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:25.910
You hear it four times a day.

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:26.910
You look at hacker news.

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:28.284
People talk about
it a lot and we

00:05:28.284 --> 00:05:31.500
have a lot of customers
who are implementing them.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:34.630
We didn't use the term
microservices at Google,

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:38.700
but at Google we've been
doing this for a decade or so.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.440
And quite famously,
internally at Google,

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:41.880
we have a flat network.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:44.130
We don't protect resources
from other resources

00:05:44.130 --> 00:05:44.850
via the network.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.840
Everything can address
everything else.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:51.330
However, every single call
that we make on our network

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:54.270
is made with
authentication tokens

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:58.260
so that we can identify which
service is making a call,

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:04.489
and we can validate yes, that
is a caller that I trust.

00:06:04.489 --> 00:06:06.280
And the other time you
might be doing this,

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.190
and something we're seeing
a lot with endpoints today,

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:14.140
is customers who are not just
developing services in Google,

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:18.790
and maybe GCE, maybe GKE,
GAE, and not just in Google,

00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:20.860
but they're also running
things on premises

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:22.360
and they're developing
services that

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:24.130
need to contact each
other, whether it's

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:28.720
calling from GCP back to
their on premises services,

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:34.000
or calling from their on
premises services up to GCE.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.190
Some people attempt to
put security in here

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:38.590
with networking, but that
can be very difficult.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:40.870
And if you are
exposing IPs, it's

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:44.526
critical that you can
authenticate those calls.

00:06:44.526 --> 00:06:46.150
And it gets more
complicated than this,

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:48.340
and it's almost hard
to believe but I'm

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.710
told that we have customers
who are running services

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:53.350
in the middle of the Amazon.

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:55.330
Sounds unbelievable,
but it's true.

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:59.110
And again, there, it can
be more difficult to solve

00:06:59.110 --> 00:07:02.680
this security problem with
any sort of networking issue.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:05.680
Setting up firewalls
that can help you

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:08.080
cloud to cloud and on
premise is very difficult.

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:10.030
So it's just another
situation where

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:14.050
you want to be able authenticate
who your callers are.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:16.930
So that's a bunch of reasons
why you need service to service

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:18.820
authentication today.

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:21.100
Now I want to talk a little
bit about Cloud Endpoints

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:24.170
before Sep shows us how it
can solve all your problems.

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:27.550
So how many people have used
Cloud Endpoints in the past two

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:28.240
years?

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:30.460
Well, you might know it
as a feature of App Engine

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:32.501
because that's what it
was for a couple of years.

00:07:32.501 --> 00:07:35.146
It was a Java framework
and a Python framework

00:07:35.146 --> 00:07:36.520
that you could
use in App Engine.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:39.730
It had a few different features,
but was limited to Java,

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:41.380
Python, and App Engine.

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:44.590
Well, ever since last fall,
and now generally available,

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:46.870
we have really added a
lot to the feature set.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.480
And first of all, we
made it so it's not just

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:51.970
an App Engine feature anymore.

00:07:51.970 --> 00:07:53.770
This is a product
in cloud that can

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:55.990
be used with any of
our compute backends.

00:07:55.990 --> 00:07:58.000
And it's not just used
as an API framework,

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:00.730
but it's got these additional
features that let you protect,

00:08:00.730 --> 00:08:03.970
monitor, and scale your APIs.

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:07.840
So one of the key components is
what we call a distributed API

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:09.850
gateway, and this
is an architecture

00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:11.620
that we've been
using at Google now

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:14.080
for a couple of
years for our APIs.

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:16.780
We used to have an API
proxy and every call

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:20.285
that you made to
www.googleapis.com

00:08:20.285 --> 00:08:21.160
went through a proxy.

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:24.430
And that did things like
authentication, and monitoring,

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:28.480
and logging, and we used all
those systems internally.

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:33.070
But we found that the extra
network hop added latency

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:35.860
and dealing with lots
and lots of configuration

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:38.510
that this thing had to know
about was difficult as well.

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:41.860
So we moved to an architecture
where our proxy is actually

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:45.520
a very thin proxy that gets
deployed with each backend.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:48.549
So instead of having a fat
proxy that sits in the middle

00:08:48.549 --> 00:08:50.590
and does a bunch of work
and then forwards calls,

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:53.770
we deploy, in a container
with each backend, the proxy.

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:56.140
So calls can get routed
directly to the backend

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:58.720
and the proxy can perform
that same work there.

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:02.240
We find that it's very, very
performant, and very scalable.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:05.020
Endpoints is an open source.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:08.380
It's based on Nginx, which
is an open sourced proxy

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:11.620
and uses that same architecture.

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:16.300
It sits on top of the same
services we use for our APIs.

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:20.200
Endpoints is also deeply
integrated with GCP.

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:23.260
So if you're using our
App Engine frameworks,

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:23.990
it's built in.

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:25.990
If you're using App Engine
Flexible Environment,

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:27.730
our proxy is built in.

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:29.590
If you're using GKE,
we have a container

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:31.280
that you can drop
into your deployment,

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:33.850
and of course, you can take our
proxy and drop it on GCE too.

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:37.750
So it's deeply integrated
with the backend.

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:40.390
It also sends all of
your monitoring metrics

00:09:40.390 --> 00:09:43.060
to Google Cloud Console
where you have dashboards.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:46.220
It sends all of your logs
to Stackdriver logging.

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:47.950
It uses Stackdriver Trace.

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:50.290
For people who are
developing on Google

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:54.280
and developing new applications,
whatever language you're using,

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:58.730
it can add these features that
we're going to show today.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:01.190
So we're talking about
authentication today,

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:03.340
and as I mentioned, it
also does good logging.

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:04.840
And I think Sep is
going to show you

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:08.610
a little bit about what the
monitoring looks like later on.

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:14.410
And it's very, very scalable,
and it's also very performant.

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:16.480
We find that, at
scale, it tends to add

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:21.700
less than a millisecond of
latency to your API requests.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:26.170
Many competing solutions add one
or even two orders of magnitude

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:28.460
more than that.

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:31.100
And again, to emphasize, this
is not an App Engine feature

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:32.180
anymore.

00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:36.230
You can use it with any language
on any of our compute backends.

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:40.990
Google functions coming soonish.

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:43.600
So this is kind of what the
architecture looks like.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.426
You can place your
calls from anywhere.

00:10:45.426 --> 00:10:47.050
Here, we show them
from an Android app,

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:50.110
an iOS app, a browser.

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:51.850
Of course, it can
be another service,

00:10:51.850 --> 00:10:55.660
but all of your web traffic
is routed through this proxy.

00:10:55.660 --> 00:10:58.289
Its an Nginx based
proxy, and that's

00:10:58.289 --> 00:10:59.830
the proxy that
actually does the work

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:02.860
and decides whether or
not a call gets through.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:06.400
That thing receives config from
a service we call the Google

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:08.140
Service Management API.

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:10.630
And then at runtime its checking
in with something called

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:13.460
the service control API, and
that's the thing that's saying,

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:16.240
hey, is this API key
valid, or can this project

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:17.950
call your project.

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:20.830
And its also reporting your
metrics to Google Cloud logging

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:24.370
and that's the thing that sends
the data up to the dashboard.

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:26.960
When calls go through they
get forward to your backend,

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:29.310
and if a call is rejected,
say it's not authenticated,

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:30.560
it never gets to your backend.

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:37.210
About a year and a half ago
when we really started saying,

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:40.590
hey, we want to handle any
language, it was clear to us

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:43.840
that we needed an IDL that
could describe these APIs.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:45.990
When we stepped away
from the framework world,

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:50.430
we need a way for you to tell us
what your API is no matter what

00:11:50.430 --> 00:11:52.180
language you're writing it.

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:55.710
And so we looked around
at the different IDLs that

00:11:55.710 --> 00:11:58.380
existed at that time and
we saw that Swagger really

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:00.330
had a lot of traction,
and we decided

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:02.310
that we would adopt
Swagger for this project.

00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:05.146
And just about that time that
we started working with them,

00:12:05.146 --> 00:12:06.770
they announced that
they were forming--

00:12:06.770 --> 00:12:08.951
or they told us that they
were forming the open API

00:12:08.951 --> 00:12:09.450
initiative.

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:12.060
So we became one of the
founding members of the open API

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:15.180
initiative, along with a lot
of other industry heavyweights,

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:19.680
and are now brothers
in arms apogee to join

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:23.040
this initiative that owns
the open API specification.

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:25.500
So we're using
the open API spec,

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:27.840
not just to describe
the surface of your API,

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:32.040
but also the config for how
you want us to manage it.

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:36.100
So you use extensions in
the open API spec to say,

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:39.650
hey, these methods need
auth, or use an API key here,

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:40.650
and we really like that.

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:44.790
We really think it's a
powerful and kind of imperative

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:48.810
way for you to describe the way
you want us to manage your API.

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:50.700
We also work with gRPC.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:53.430
Anyone here using gRPC yet?

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:53.930
A few.

00:12:53.930 --> 00:12:55.880
Anyone heard about gRPC?

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:59.830
This transport that we're
using heavily for our own APIs?

00:12:59.830 --> 00:13:01.700
Well, if you are using
gRPC, then you'll

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:03.710
find that there's
probably really

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:07.850
only one proxy out
there that is really

00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:09.740
doing API management with it.

00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:12.680
You can define your
API in proto for gRPC,

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:15.260
and then you give us a yaml that
describes the configuration,

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:18.900
again, how you want us to
manage it, what sort of auth

00:13:18.900 --> 00:13:23.820
you want, and we'll manage
your gRPC APIs as well.

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:26.480
Speaking of standards,
for authentication

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:29.420
we've chosen JSON Web
Tokens, which are pronounced

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:33.200
"Jot" for non-obvious reasons.

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:36.950
But JWTs and open ID
connect has kind of

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:38.780
become a standard
for authentication,

00:13:38.780 --> 00:13:41.750
and that's where we're using
for authentication as well.

00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:45.554
We've got a couple of providers
that we're working with.

00:13:45.554 --> 00:13:46.220
It's a standard.

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:47.970
You can write them
yourself, but if you're

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:52.975
using Firebase or Auth0,
we can validate those.

00:13:52.975 --> 00:13:54.600
And that's all I
wanted to get through,

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:56.280
and now I want to
hand this over to Sep,

00:13:56.280 --> 00:13:58.185
who's going to show us
how the whole thing works.

00:13:58.185 --> 00:13:58.560
SEP EBRAHIMZADEH: Great.

00:13:58.560 --> 00:13:59.390
Thanks, Dan.

00:13:59.390 --> 00:14:01.140
So as Dan mentioned,
