00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:39.630
of languages or frameworks.

00:16:39.630 --> 00:16:41.500
So that's all with the code.

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:46.082
Let's go back and think about
from the client's perspective,

00:16:46.082 --> 00:16:47.790
what we need to
communicate to the client

00:16:47.790 --> 00:16:50.100
to be able to call this API.

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:53.700
We are surely not going to
share the Node.js implementation

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:55.110
code with them.

00:16:55.110 --> 00:16:57.570
And any documentation we
provide them is probably

00:16:57.570 --> 00:17:00.090
going to be out of
date and not convey

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:04.079
the full message of what's in
the contents of the request,

00:17:04.079 --> 00:17:06.420
what they should expect
in the responses.

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:08.740
For that, we will use open API.

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:13.380
The open API spec for this
simple API that has one method

00:17:13.380 --> 00:17:14.940
is also rather simple.

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:19.329
It has one path for
the echo method.

00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:22.270
It shows that it's
a post method.

00:17:22.270 --> 00:17:26.050
And then it has some
documentation specific fields

00:17:26.050 --> 00:17:32.050
like description that can
be parsed by open API tools

00:17:32.050 --> 00:17:35.800
like the Swagger
UI, and it can also

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:41.200
be used to generate client
libraries for calling this API.

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:45.170
The rest of it is about what
the responses would look like.

00:17:45.170 --> 00:17:50.250
A 200 response is defined
to have this type of message

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:52.174
in it, an echo message.

00:17:52.174 --> 00:17:53.590
And the echo message
at the bottom

00:17:53.590 --> 00:17:58.120
is defined here as a
message that has a field

00:17:58.120 --> 00:18:01.076
and that field will have
a string value on it.

00:18:01.076 --> 00:18:02.200
So you know, rather simple.

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.930
I just want to draw your
attention to the host field,

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:05.790
here.

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:10.300
I've named the API,
echo-api-without-auth.

00:18:10.300 --> 00:18:14.980
At the endpoints, keyword
after is a reserved keyword.

00:18:14.980 --> 00:18:19.130
Then you see my project ID,
the Google Cloud project ID.

00:18:19.130 --> 00:18:23.280
So the name of this
APIs unique because it's

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:26.560
scoped into the project
IDs that are unique

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:29.680
and I can have multiple
different endpoints to APIs

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:30.990
in one project.

00:18:30.990 --> 00:18:32.740
DAN CIRULI: If I want
to, can I serve this

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:33.819
from a different URL?

00:18:33.819 --> 00:18:35.110
SEP EBRAHIMZADEH: You can, yes.

