00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:05.020
you on-prem data centers,
and endpoint should

00:19:05.020 --> 00:19:07.360
work in all of those scenarios.

00:19:07.360 --> 00:19:14.127
So let's go out of this open API
spec and run a gcloud command

00:19:14.127 --> 00:19:15.460
and gcloud, as I'm sure you're--

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:17.221
who's used gcloud before?

00:19:17.221 --> 00:19:17.720
Great.

00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:19.303
Now, gcloud, as I'm
sure you're aware,

00:19:19.303 --> 00:19:24.340
is the CLI for almost all
of Google Cloud products

00:19:24.340 --> 00:19:27.580
and I'll be running
this gcloud command that

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:31.570
uploads the open API
specification to the control

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:32.860
plane for endpoints.

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:35.950
So I'm running gcloud
service-management deploy

00:19:35.950 --> 00:19:37.330
and an openapi.yaml.

00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:40.915
So what's happening
right now is the open API

00:19:40.915 --> 00:19:44.210
is sent to the control plane.

00:19:44.210 --> 00:19:46.710
The control plane takes a
look at it and validates it.

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:50.890
So it looks for any mistakes
in it, inconsistencies,

00:19:50.890 --> 00:19:55.120
it also compares it to a
style guide that we have

00:19:55.120 --> 00:19:57.880
and we've published as
the style guide for how

00:19:57.880 --> 00:20:02.170
to design your APIs so that
they're hopefully future proof

00:20:02.170 --> 00:20:04.450
and they're consistent.

00:20:04.450 --> 00:20:08.800
And after it's done persisting
this copy of the open API spec,

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:12.480
it will return a unique
identifier for that copy of it.

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:16.480
In case I go to make changes to
my local copy of the open API

00:20:16.480 --> 00:20:19.210
spec, this immutable
instance of it

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:21.880
is persisted on
the control plane.

