that Sep showed

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:38.610
has some graphs that looks
similar to what you see in App

00:24:38.610 --> 00:24:40.440
Engine, but what he
showed at the bottom

00:24:40.440 --> 00:24:42.570
is it's all broken
down by method.

00:24:42.570 --> 00:24:45.370
So you can not just see,
hey, the 98th percentile

00:24:45.370 --> 00:24:47.280
say for our API is high.

00:24:47.280 --> 00:24:49.770
You might see there's just
one particular method that

00:24:49.770 --> 00:24:53.520
has a latency that's
high or that's

00:24:53.520 --> 00:24:55.080
getting increased
the error rates.

00:24:55.080 --> 00:24:57.500
So it's giving you
a higher fidelity

00:24:57.500 --> 00:25:00.510
of metrics and a
higher fidelity of logs

00:25:00.510 --> 00:25:02.699
because we know about your API.

00:25:02.699 --> 00:25:03.990
SEP EBRAHIMZADEH: Exactly that.

00:25:03.990 --> 00:25:05.490
So that's pretty cool.

00:25:05.490 --> 00:25:08.026
We just uploaded our
open API specification,

00:25:08.026 --> 00:25:09.900
which we were going to
write anyways in order

00:25:09.900 --> 00:25:13.370
to communicate the surface
of our API to our clients,

00:25:13.370 --> 00:25:18.460
and we got monitoring
and logging effortlessly.

00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:20.520
But the focus of the
talk is authentication.

00:25:20.520 --> 00:25:23.970
As you noticed, I made the
curl command from my laptop,

00:25:23.970 --> 00:25:26.700
and this API is open
on the public internet.

00:25:26.700 --> 00:25:29.100
Whoever has this URL
can go call it now.

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:30.566
So how do I secure it?

00:25:30.566 --> 00:25:32.190
I have multiple
options for doing that.

00:25:32.190 --> 00:25:36.270
I could go and write a lot
of Note.js code in order

00:25:36.270 --> 00:25:39.090
to expect authentication
tokens and the requests that

00:25:39.090 --> 00:25:42.210
are coming in, and
basically verify

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:44.820
the signature of those
authentication tokens,

00:25:44.820 --> 00:25:47.620
and do all the hard work myself.

00:25:47.620 --> 00:25:51.690
Or I can configure endpoints
to expect and require

00:25:51.690 --> 00:25:56.640
authentication for my API, and
not make any backend Node.js

00:25:56.640 --> 00:25:57.540
changes.

00:25:57.540 --> 00:26:00.990
So which one seems
like the easier path?

00:26:00.990 --> 00:26:05.670
Let's take a look at
the other open API

00:26:05.670 --> 00:26:08.640
file that I have here to
illustrate how many lines

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:12.370
we've added, and the first
section is all the same.

00:26:12.370 --> 00:26:15.090
The only thing I've changed
is the name of the API,

00:26:15.090 --> 00:26:18.460
which is to say it's
echo-api-with-auth this time.

00:26:18.460 --> 00:26:20.930
The rest is all the same,
except at the bottom

00:26:20.930 --> 00:26:23.100
I've added about 10 lines.

00:26:23.100 --> 00:26:24.320
The lines I've added--

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:26.100
first, there's a
security definition,

00:26:26.100 --> 00:26:30.240
which is an open API
concept, and the name I've

00:26:30.240 --> 00:26:32.990
given it is google_id_token.

00:26:32.990 --> 00:26:34.850
And I've given it a
few different fields.

00:26:34.850 --> 00:26:37.290
The important one to call
out here is that for the demo

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:39.870
I'm using the Google
identity provider,

00:26:39.870 --> 00:26:44.090
and the issuer I'm configuring
is accounts.google.com.

00:26:44.090 --> 00:26:47.610
Now, we will talk about other
identity providers and identity

00:26:47.610 --> 00:26:50.940
provider hubs like
Auth0, Firebase,

00:26:50.940 --> 00:26:53.100
and other third parties
that we support,

00:26:53.100 --> 00:26:58.397
as well as custom JWT after
this part of the demo.

00:26:58.397 --> 00:27:00.480
The second part, if you're
familiar with open API,

00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:05.070
is simply saying that this
google_id_token security

00:27:05.070 --> 00:27:07.290
definition should
be required for all

00:27:07.290 --> 00:27:09.990
the methods in this API.

00:27:09.990 --> 00:27:12.780
Alternatively, I could
have required just some

00:27:12.780 --> 00:27:15.980
of the methods to have
authentication and keep

00:27:15.980 --> 00:27:20.640
the rest either open, or
have multiple security

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:24.480
definitions here and say these
methods require that security

00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:27.990
definition, those other methods
require another security

00:27:27.990 --> 00:27:28.995
definition.

00:27:28.995 --> 00:27:30.870
So we'll talk about why
that might be useful.

00:27:30.870 --> 00:27:35.190
But let's exit this and do
the same thing we did before,

00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:36.910
gcloud service-management.

00:27:40.310 --> 00:27:46.420
Deploy this open API
specification to the endpoints

00:27:46.420 --> 00:27:47.230
control plane.

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:50.210
At the end, so after a few
seconds, once it finishes,

00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:52.360
it will give me a
configuration ID

00:27:52.360 --> 00:27:56.590
and it has persisted this
version of my open API spec.

00:27:56.590 --> 00:28:01.240
And what I'll do with that,
I'll copy the configuration ID

00:28:01.240 --> 00:28:04.330
and I'll put it in the
application.yaml file

00:28:04.330 --> 00:28:07.337
for App Engine, just
like I did previously.

00:28:07.337 --> 00:28:08.920
You'll notice of
warning message here.

00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:11.680
This is one of the
validations that it's doing.

00:28:11.680 --> 00:28:17.680
It's saying that the echo method
is not protected with API keys.

00:28:17.680 --> 00:28:21.010
It's asking if I'm sure
that I want to put this up

00:28:21.010 --> 00:28:24.240
because then it would
be publicly accessible

00:28:24.240 --> 00:28:27.250
and it doesn't know
that I have security

00:28:27.250 --> 00:28:30.520
requirements on this method
with security definitions,

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:34.450
but that's the nice thing
about having your open API

00:28:34.450 --> 00:28:35.740
spec validated.

00:28:35.740 --> 00:28:39.040
You can see any warnings and
errors associated with it.

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:42.100
So let me copy this
conflagration ID,

00:28:42.100 --> 00:28:43.660
put it in the app.yaml again.

00:28:46.490 --> 00:28:50.600
I'll comment the
echo-api-without-auth section

00:28:50.600 --> 00:28:52.925
and un-comment this section.

00:28:56.100 --> 00:28:58.810
And again, I will
do gcloud app deploy

00:28:58.810 --> 00:29:00.590
to deploy the same backend.

00:29:00.590 --> 00:29:03.976
Remember, we have not changed
the Node.js code at all.

00:29:03.976 --> 00:29:05.600
And I want to compare
them side by side

00:29:05.600 --> 00:29:09.050
so perhaps I won't promote
it to the production

00:29:09.050 --> 00:29:11.394
version of my app,
and maybe I'll

00:29:11.394 --> 00:29:12.560
give it a different version.

00:29:12.560 --> 00:29:14.030
Let's call it with-auth.

00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:19.890
And once this
finishes deploying,

00:29:19.890 --> 00:29:22.460
it will give me a different URL.

00:29:22.460 --> 00:29:25.440
The URL will be
with-auth, and the rest

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:28.680
is the same, that's why I gave
it a different version name.

00:29:28.680 --> 00:29:30.540
So I've also deployed
this because I don't

00:29:30.540 --> 00:29:32.490
want to keep you guys waiting.

00:29:32.490 --> 00:29:35.730
Let's go ahead and issue
the same curl command

00:29:35.730 --> 00:29:38.380
against this deployment.

00:29:44.440 --> 00:29:47.200
And sure enough, this time
the call didn't go through.

00:29:47.200 --> 00:29:50.170
I got an HTTP 401
error and the error

00:29:50.170 --> 00:29:53.800
has a message in it that
says JWT validation failed.

00:29:53.800 --> 00:29:56.560
Credentials were either
missing or invalid.

00:29:56.560 --> 00:29:57.490
So that's pretty cool.

00:29:57.490 --> 00:29:59.560
We just secured the API.

00:29:59.560 --> 00:30:03.040
It now requires authentication
and my work here is done.

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:03.940
We can go home.

00:30:03.940 --> 00:30:07.270
Or maybe we should also take a
look at the client side as well

00:30:07.270 --> 00:30:12.800
and see how we can actually
securely call this API now.

00:30:12.800 --> 00:30:16.100
Well, for the
client side, I will

00:30:16.100 --> 00:30:17.510
demo two different scenarios.

00:30:17.510 --> 00:30:23.830
Let's say you're running on
the Google Compute Engine

00:30:23.830 --> 00:30:26.930
and you have a virtual machine
and you're running your Python

00:30:26.930 --> 00:30:28.670
client there.

00:30:28.670 --> 00:30:30.260
And then I will show
you what it means

00:30:30.260 --> 00:30:34.220
to run it outside of the
Google Cloud platform

00:30:34.220 --> 00:30:38.280
and either run it on
prem or from my laptop.

00:30:38.280 --> 00:30:43.210
So let's go to the developer
console for Compute Engine

00:30:43.210 --> 00:30:47.870
and I have a vanilla
GCE instance here,

00:30:47.870 --> 00:30:49.800
and it's just sitting
there doing nothing.

00:30:49.800 --> 00:30:52.880
So that's why GCE is
recommending that I can either

00:30:52.880 --> 00:30:56.240
downsize it and save some money,
or improve its performance.

00:30:56.240 --> 00:30:57.820
And let's SSH to this.

00:30:57.820 --> 00:31:00.320
DAN CIRULI: This is one of my
favorite features, by the way,

00:31:00.320 --> 00:31:03.830
of Cloud Console, is the
click the button to SSH in.

00:31:03.830 --> 00:31:05.690
SEP EBRAHIMZADEH: Right.

00:31:05.690 --> 00:31:08.510
And the nice thing about
running from a GCE instance

00:31:08.510 --> 00:31:12.140
is that for making my
authenticated call,

00:31:12.140 --> 00:31:17.000
I can take advantage of the
built in credential management

00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:21.230
features that all of the
compute platforms on GCP have.

00:31:21.230 --> 00:31:23.750
So I have a service
account, just

00:31:23.750 --> 00:31:28.100
like the accounts that you and
I would have as human users.

00:31:28.100 --> 00:31:30.140
The service account
has some credentials.

00:31:30.140 --> 00:31:32.590
It has a private key
associated with it.

00:31:32.590 --> 00:31:37.670
On this GCE virtual
machine, those private keys

00:31:37.670 --> 00:31:40.010
for the service accounts
are readily available,

00:31:40.010 --> 00:31:42.290
and I don't have to
worry about security

00:31:42.290 --> 00:31:45.890
copying them around, or
rotating them, and so on.

00:31:45.890 --> 00:31:49.880
Let's take a look at this
GCE client code that I have.

00:31:49.880 --> 00:31:52.520
It's just a few
lines of Python code,

00:31:52.520 --> 00:31:56.850
and hopefully it's
legible on the screen.

00:31:56.850 --> 00:32:01.400
This is the service account that
it's using the credentials of.

00:32:01.400 --> 00:32:06.560
Its next demo service account
at some URL that we have.

00:32:06.560 --> 00:32:12.570
What it's doing is first it
is creating a JSON web token,

00:32:12.570 --> 00:32:14.570
and the payload for this
JSON web token is here.

00:32:14.570 --> 00:32:18.900
It has an issue at field
and an expiration field.

00:32:18.900 --> 00:32:23.240
The important ones are that the
issuer is the service account.

00:32:23.240 --> 00:32:27.860
The target audience is the
echo-api-with-auth endpoint

00:32:27.860 --> 00:32:30.419
service that I want to call.

00:32:30.419 --> 00:32:31.835
And then what the
code is doing is

00:32:31.835 --> 00:32:35.570
it's signing this JWT with
the credentials of the service

00:32:35.570 --> 00:32:40.280
account that it has access
to on the virtual machine.

00:32:40.280 --> 00:32:46.660
Then it uses that signed_JWT
to call the Google ID provider

00:32:46.660 --> 00:32:48.490
and get an ID token.

00:32:48.490 --> 00:32:51.760
The ID token is
also another JWT.

00:32:51.760 --> 00:32:56.200
It uses that JWT to
call the echo API.

00:32:56.200 --> 00:32:58.660
So we'll talk about why
there is a two hop step here

00:32:58.660 --> 00:33:01.195
and what the advantages and
disadvantages of doing that

00:33:01.195 --> 00:33:02.246
are.

00:33:02.246 --> 00:33:03.620
Let's go ahead
and test the code.

00:33:06.720 --> 00:33:10.100
First, without authentication,
sure enough, we

00:33:10.100 --> 00:33:12.890
get the HTTP 401 error and
I've added a simple flag here

00:33:12.890 --> 00:33:16.850
that says auth is
true or yes, and it

00:33:16.850 --> 00:33:18.670
is creating a signed_JWT.

00:33:18.670 --> 00:33:22.260
It is obtaining an ID
token with that signed_JWT

00:33:22.260 --> 00:33:25.280
and then it's calling the
echo API with that ID token.

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:27.340
Sure enough, there
request succeeds,

00:33:27.340 --> 00:33:29.587
and it returns the
message that had sent it.

00:33:29.587 --> 00:33:32.170
DAN CIRULI: And that and that's
kind of cool that had happened

00:33:32.170 --> 00:33:35.000
is it is using its
credential management that's

00:33:35.000 --> 00:33:36.860
available within GCE--

00:33:36.860 --> 00:33:38.640
any of the compute platforms.

00:33:38.640 --> 00:33:41.250
So you're not having
to maintain a secrets,

00:33:41.250 --> 00:33:43.591
you're not having to
rotate keys, that's just

00:33:43.591 --> 00:33:44.840
going to happen automatically.

00:33:44.840 --> 00:33:46.370
You sign that JWT,
and it's going

00:33:46.370 --> 00:33:47.953
to use the latest
credential, and that

00:33:47.953 --> 00:33:49.400
will be validated by Google.

00:33:49.400 --> 00:33:50.610
SEP EBRAHIMZADEH: Right.

00:33:50.610 --> 00:33:52.610
And you might say,
well, I don't want

00:33:52.610 --> 00:33:56.010
to run my Python client side
on a Google Compute Engine

00:33:56.010 --> 00:33:56.510
instance.

00:33:56.510 --> 00:33:58.550
I want to run it elsewhere.

00:33:58.550 --> 00:34:00.540
You can do that too.

00:34:00.540 --> 00:34:03.710
Let's go to take a look
at the service account

00:34:03.710 --> 00:34:07.630
that I just used in the
Google Cloud Console.

00:34:07.630 --> 00:34:09.889
So under the service
accounts page,

00:34:09.889 --> 00:34:13.610
I have the next demo service
account that we just saw,

00:34:13.610 --> 00:34:16.090
and I can create a key for it.

00:34:16.090 --> 00:34:18.710
When I create the
key, the credentials

00:34:18.710 --> 00:34:22.330
are downloaded to my machine.

00:34:22.330 --> 00:34:24.530
Let's tale a look
at the credentials

00:34:24.530 --> 00:34:27.389
and you'll see that it
has a private key in it.

00:34:27.389 --> 00:34:29.630
So this is the
private key portion

00:34:29.630 --> 00:34:34.190
and from this point on I will
be responsible for securely

00:34:34.190 --> 00:34:35.900
distributing it to
any place that I

00:34:35.900 --> 00:34:39.920
want to run my clients on,
and rotating it, and so on.

00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:46.219
So let's close that and
let's go back to my prompt.

00:34:46.219 --> 00:34:48.010
DAN CIRULI: And this
is your laptop prompt?

00:34:48.010 --> 00:34:48.980
SEP EBRAHIMZADEH: And
this is my laptop.

00:34:48.980 --> 00:34:50.250
DAN CIRULI: You're not
running in Compute Engine.

00:34:50.250 --> 00:34:51.333
SEP EBRAHIMZADEH: Exactly.

00:34:51.333 --> 00:34:53.310
This is outside of the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:34:53.310 --> 00:34:56.250
It's on the Wi-Fi
that we have here.

00:34:56.250 --> 00:35:02.520
And I have a slightly different
client code than in Python

00:35:02.520 --> 00:35:04.390
for running locally.

00:35:04.390 --> 00:35:06.020
Let's take a look at that.

00:35:06.020 --> 00:35:08.430
Again, it creates a JWT.

00:35:08.430 --> 00:35:10.350
The only difference
is this time it

00:35:10.350 --> 00:35:13.050
signs the JWT with
the credentials

00:35:13.050 --> 00:35:16.040
that I have locally
on my machine.

00:35:16.040 --> 00:35:18.860
So I have the private key
in the service account

00:35:18.860 --> 00:35:21.470
file that I will
pass into the script.

00:35:21.470 --> 00:35:23.870
It signs the JWT with that.

00:35:23.870 --> 00:35:27.840
It uses that signed_JWT
to get an ID token

00:35:27.840 --> 00:35:32.414
and then it calls the
echo API that ID token.

00:35:32.414 --> 00:35:34.080
DAN CIRULI: So the
thing to bear in mind

00:35:34.080 --> 00:35:35.704
here is you can run
this code anywhere.

00:35:35.704 --> 00:35:37.962
If you're calling from
on prem to Google,

00:35:37.962 --> 00:35:40.170
you're calling from another
cloud provider to Google,

00:35:40.170 --> 00:35:42.780
you have to get that key
there and you probably

00:35:42.780 --> 00:35:45.210
have methods that you're
using for handling secrets

00:35:45.210 --> 00:35:48.720
in each of those places,
but this file now, it's

00:35:48.720 --> 00:35:50.070
an important file.

00:35:50.070 --> 00:35:52.830
Anyone who has this has the
ability to call your service.

00:35:52.830 --> 00:35:56.190
So on Google, you don't have
to worry about that when you're

00:35:56.190 --> 00:35:57.420
calling, when your service--

00:35:57.420 --> 00:36:00.220
what he's running in Python--
is running outside of Google,

00:36:00.220 --> 00:36:03.270
you don't to
maintain the privacy

00:36:03.270 --> 00:36:04.647
and secrecy of this file.

00:36:04.647 --> 00:36:05.730
SEP EBRAHIMZADEH: Exactly.

00:36:05.730 --> 00:36:09.520
So let's go ahead and test
this one out and sure enough,

00:36:09.520 --> 00:36:10.530
the same thing.

00:36:10.530 --> 00:36:12.060
If I don't give it
any credentials,

00:36:12.060 --> 00:36:14.940
the call fails with an HTTP 401.

00:36:14.940 --> 00:36:22.240
If I give it this service
account credentials file,

00:36:22.240 --> 00:36:24.490
it will create a
signed_JWT, get an ID token,

00:36:24.490 --> 00:36:26.980
and call the API
with that ID token.

00:36:26.980 --> 00:36:30.190
So that's pretty cool,
but it shows the advantage

00:36:30.190 --> 00:36:34.250
and disadvantage of running
your client side on the Compute

00:36:34.250 --> 00:36:39.860
Engine, or App Engine, or
basically the other computer

00:36:39.860 --> 00:36:43.660
platform that we have on GCP.

00:36:43.660 --> 00:36:48.370
So, we can go back to
the endpoints dashboard,

00:36:48.370 --> 00:36:55.550
and see the same graphs, and see
the APIs running side by side.

00:36:55.550 --> 00:36:58.150
This is the endpoints
API with authentication

00:36:58.150 --> 00:37:00.240
and I've been generating
some traffic against it.

00:37:00.240 --> 00:37:02.560
So this one will
also have metrics

00:37:02.560 --> 00:37:05.200
and it also shows
the 400 requests

00:37:05.200 --> 00:37:09.967
that were rejected because they
didn't have authentication.

00:37:09.967 --> 00:37:12.050
So with that, let's go
back to the slides, please.

00:37:16.330 --> 00:37:17.960
So what did we just look at?

00:37:17.960 --> 00:37:21.150
First, we looked at some
sample Node.js code,

00:37:21.150 --> 00:37:22.740
and the code might
have easily been

00:37:22.740 --> 00:37:25.320
written in a different language
or a different framework.

00:37:25.320 --> 00:37:27.600
We showed that it
implemented a rest API

00:37:27.600 --> 00:37:30.780
and we showed how easy it
was to write an open API

00:37:30.780 --> 00:37:35.610
specification that describes the
methods, requests, responses,

00:37:35.610 --> 00:37:38.900
and documentation for that API.

00:37:38.900 --> 00:37:41.250
And that if you have
the open API spec,

00:37:41.250 --> 00:37:44.880
you basically have an endpoints
API, and you can get metrics,

00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:49.290
logs, and authentication
out of Endpoints for that.

00:37:49.290 --> 00:37:51.390
And of course, we
saw that the calls

00:37:51.390 --> 00:37:55.290
we made before we added
authentication succeeded.

00:37:55.290 --> 00:37:58.350
Once we added authentication,
the unauthenticated

00:37:58.350 --> 00:38:02.430
calls with curl, or with the
clients written in Python,

00:38:02.430 --> 00:38:03.690
failed.

00:38:03.690 --> 00:38:10.020
And the key point here is that
the response that was HTTP 401

00:38:10.020 --> 00:38:13.770
came from the endpoints
extensible server proxy.

00:38:13.770 --> 00:38:19.180
So those requests never actually
made it to the Node.js packet.

00:38:19.180 --> 00:38:23.540
And of course, when we had
authentication JSON web tokens

00:38:23.540 --> 00:38:28.200
the request succeeded and
the response was proxied back

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:31.536
from the Node.js backend.

00:38:31.536 --> 00:38:32.910
The few lines of
code that we had

00:38:32.910 --> 00:38:34.500
to the few lines
of configuration--

00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:38.100
excuse me-- that we had to
add to the open API spec,

00:38:38.100 --> 00:38:40.440
I'll repeat it here,
the issuer we chose

00:38:40.440 --> 00:38:43.560
was the Google ID token issuer.

00:38:43.560 --> 00:38:44.730
Now, why did we do that?

00:38:44.730 --> 00:38:47.820
We did that so that any
service account on the project

00:38:47.820 --> 00:38:51.336
that I was using would
be able to call the API.

00:38:51.336 --> 00:38:52.710
You might say,
well, I don't want

00:38:52.710 --> 00:38:54.750
to use the Google ID provider.

00:38:54.750 --> 00:38:57.750
I might want to use a third
party provider like Auth0

00:38:57.750 --> 00:38:59.730
or I want to sign my own JWTs.

00:38:59.730 --> 00:39:03.570
Well, that's also possible
with the same configuration.

00:39:03.570 --> 00:39:07.230
You just have to provide
two extra lines that

00:39:07.230 --> 00:39:14.610
tell the extensible server proxy
where to get your JWT public

00:39:14.610 --> 00:39:18.790
key and what the audiences
are for your JSON web tokens.

00:39:21.590 --> 00:39:23.700
Now, the advantage
of doing this is

00:39:23.700 --> 00:39:28.410
that you're no longer calling
the Google ID provider at all,

00:39:28.410 --> 00:39:31.950
that you can create a signed
JWT and call your API directly

00:39:31.950 --> 00:39:33.100
with that.

00:39:33.100 --> 00:39:35.130
The disadvantage
is that anytime you

00:39:35.130 --> 00:39:38.510
want to change the audiences,
or rotate your credentials,

00:39:38.510 --> 00:39:41.540
or say change the
public key URI,

00:39:41.540 --> 00:39:45.570
you would need to
redeploy your backend.

00:39:45.570 --> 00:39:47.550
Let's look at the
differences between them.

00:39:47.550 --> 00:39:49.260
The cool thing is
that you could have,

00:39:49.260 --> 00:39:53.040
as I mentioned earlier, multiple
different security definitions

00:39:53.040 --> 00:39:55.590
if you're using your
own signed JWTs.

00:39:55.590 --> 00:39:59.055
And that way do a bit
of authorization too

00:39:59.055 --> 00:40:03.660
to say the JSON web tokens
that have this audience

00:40:03.660 --> 00:40:06.320
can only call these
methods in my API,

00:40:06.320 --> 00:40:09.390
the JSON web tokens that
have those other audiences

00:40:09.390 --> 00:40:13.950
can call all of my methods or
these other methods of my API.

00:40:13.950 --> 00:40:17.000
And of course, the advantage
of running with Google ID token

00:40:17.000 --> 00:40:19.630
and on GCP is that the
credential management,

00:40:19.630 --> 00:40:23.040
that rotating of secrets,
is handled for you.

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:24.990
So with that, I'll
hand back to Dan,

00:40:24.990 --> 00:40:27.840
who will talk about
Firebase, and Auth0,

00:40:27.840 --> 00:40:30.510
and the other features
that Endpoints is planning.

00:40:30.510 --> 00:40:32.070
DAN CIRULI: Amazing demo, Sep.

00:40:32.070 --> 00:40:33.180
Amazing.

00:40:33.180 --> 00:40:34.346
SEP EBRAHIMZADEH: Thank you.

