00:41:01.845 --> 00:41:05.674
do when calling cloud to cloud
and calling within Google.

00:41:05.674 --> 00:41:07.840
So we've talked a lot about
service to service auth,

00:41:07.840 --> 00:41:09.950
and we talked about service
accounts, or robotic accounts

00:41:09.950 --> 00:41:10.840
as we call them.

00:41:10.840 --> 00:41:13.950
But in that very
first diagram slide,

00:41:13.950 --> 00:41:16.810
I showed a mobile device.

00:41:16.810 --> 00:41:19.480
When a call is coming in
from that mobile device, what

00:41:19.480 --> 00:41:21.446
you generally want to
know there is who's

00:41:21.446 --> 00:41:22.570
holding that mobile device?

00:41:22.570 --> 00:41:25.210
Who logged into that mobile app.

00:41:25.210 --> 00:41:27.460
You guys have heard
a lot about Firebase.

00:41:27.460 --> 00:41:29.345
Firebase is, of
course, a great company

00:41:29.345 --> 00:41:30.970
that we acquired a
couple of years ago,

00:41:30.970 --> 00:41:33.190
and I think they've
done a really good job

00:41:33.190 --> 00:41:35.110
as an acquisition
at Google of kind

00:41:35.110 --> 00:41:37.210
of maintaining a
strong identity,

00:41:37.210 --> 00:41:39.670
and also finding a great
place within Google

00:41:39.670 --> 00:41:40.750
and integrating well.

00:41:40.750 --> 00:41:43.540
They've really integrated well
on that mobile developer--

00:41:43.540 --> 00:41:47.570
all of this and the tools
that Google is producing,

00:41:47.570 --> 00:41:50.590
they have done a good
job of consolidating them

00:41:50.590 --> 00:41:52.030
under Firebase.

00:41:52.030 --> 00:41:54.520
If you are using Firebase,
one of their features

00:41:54.520 --> 00:41:57.490
is Firebase auth, and they
have good client libraries

00:41:57.490 --> 00:42:01.450
that you can use in mobile
devices or web apps, that

00:42:01.450 --> 00:42:02.980
let your users sign in.

00:42:02.980 --> 00:42:05.795
And your users can sign in
with username and password,

00:42:05.795 --> 00:42:08.170
they can choose their Google
auth, they can use Facebook,

00:42:08.170 --> 00:42:10.984
or Twitter, a bunch of
different identity providers.

00:42:10.984 --> 00:42:13.150
They have a good client
library experience for that.

00:42:13.150 --> 00:42:15.280
Well, if you are using
Firebase and you'd

00:42:15.280 --> 00:42:17.290
like to put some
logic and say run it

00:42:17.290 --> 00:42:20.860
on App Engine or any of
our compute backends,

00:42:20.860 --> 00:42:23.920
they of course, are sending
a JWT token as well,

00:42:23.920 --> 00:42:26.950
and of course, endpoints
can validate that JWT token.

00:42:26.950 --> 00:42:30.870
There's a third party
identity solution called Auth0

00:42:30.870 --> 00:42:33.100
that lots of mobile
developers are using.

00:42:33.100 --> 00:42:35.050
We work with them as well.

00:42:35.050 --> 00:42:38.080
Configuration there, very,
very similar because it's

00:42:38.080 --> 00:42:39.130
all JWT based.

00:42:39.130 --> 00:42:41.970
You basically tell us who's
going to be issuing this,

00:42:41.970 --> 00:42:44.470
and there you will put
your Firebase project ID,

00:42:44.470 --> 00:42:47.260
and you give the standard
address for where

00:42:47.260 --> 00:42:51.010
to get that public key, and we
can validate those JWT tokens.

00:42:51.010 --> 00:42:52.960
So that's the
configuration you would

00:42:52.960 --> 00:42:55.930
use for your mobile API, the
one that will be called directly

00:42:55.930 --> 00:42:59.000
by that mobile device.

00:42:59.000 --> 00:43:01.670
So that's the bulk
of the stuff that we

00:43:01.670 --> 00:43:05.300
