00:43:09.860 --> 00:43:10.794
when you might use it.

00:43:10.794 --> 00:43:12.710
Did want to give you a
glimpse of what's going

00:43:12.710 --> 00:43:14.750
to be happening coming up.

00:43:14.750 --> 00:43:16.040
We have a lot of users--

00:43:16.040 --> 00:43:19.530
we went full beta, I
think, in September.

00:43:19.530 --> 00:43:21.210
We've been GA since last month.

00:43:21.210 --> 00:43:23.180
We've got a lot of
people using this.

00:43:23.180 --> 00:43:25.850
The great thing about getting
users using your software

00:43:25.850 --> 00:43:27.570
is they start to tell
you what's missing

00:43:27.570 --> 00:43:29.630
and that's fantastic feedback.

00:43:29.630 --> 00:43:32.630
So, we've talked a lot
about authentication.

00:43:32.630 --> 00:43:35.000
Well, our users
want authorization.

00:43:35.000 --> 00:43:36.500
We have a lot of
people asking about

00:43:36.500 --> 00:43:38.270
that and so you can bet
we're thinking pretty

00:43:38.270 --> 00:43:39.650
hard about what to do about it.

00:43:39.650 --> 00:43:41.750
Also very fine control.

00:43:41.750 --> 00:43:43.970
Sep mentioned, one of
the things you can do now

00:43:43.970 --> 00:43:45.650
is you can control.

00:43:45.650 --> 00:43:48.950
You might have some methods
that you require API keys

00:43:48.950 --> 00:43:50.180
for and some not.

00:43:50.180 --> 00:43:53.120
In his configuration, he
was applying that policy

00:43:53.120 --> 00:43:54.620
to the entire API.

00:43:54.620 --> 00:43:57.500
You can do that per
method, but people

00:43:57.500 --> 00:43:59.390
want it even more fine control.

00:43:59.390 --> 00:44:02.810
We have customers who define
resources within their API

00:44:02.810 --> 00:44:05.330
and they want to control who
has access to what resource.

00:44:05.330 --> 00:44:07.250
So if I've got my
bookshelves API,

00:44:07.250 --> 00:44:09.830
maybe you get access
to the history section

00:44:09.830 --> 00:44:11.600
and you get access to
the science section,

00:44:11.600 --> 00:44:13.308
but I'm not going to
let you read history

00:44:13.308 --> 00:44:14.180
or you read science.

00:44:14.180 --> 00:44:17.360
So we're looking at ways we
might do that for our users.

00:44:17.360 --> 00:44:19.744
Rate limiting comes up a
lot, especially for people

00:44:19.744 --> 00:44:21.410
who are starting to
think about exposing

00:44:21.410 --> 00:44:24.084
their APIs to third parties.

00:44:24.084 --> 00:44:26.000
It's not such a big deal
with your mobile app.

00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:31.140
You rarely want to deny
requests to your own API,

00:44:31.140 --> 00:44:33.620
but when other people
start using your APIs,

00:44:33.620 --> 00:44:36.530
then you do think about
denying your requests.

00:44:36.530 --> 00:44:39.072
We have a lot of other features
that people are asking for

00:44:39.072 --> 00:44:40.530
and that we're
starting to work on.

00:44:40.530 --> 00:44:43.310
So this is an area that we're
